3. Mathematical Framework for Biosphere Architecture

3.1 The Ecological State Space as a Networked Compartmental Manifold

A closed biosphere exchanges energy but not matter with its environment. Instead of treating the habitat as a single well‑mixed vessel, we partition it into a finite set of interconnected compartments \mathcal{C} = \{1,\dots,N\}, each representing a physically distinct volume (e.g., crew quarters, hydroponic bay, waste processor). Let x^{(\ell)}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^{n}_+ be the state vector in compartment \ell (species, nutrients, gases). The full system state is

\mathbf{x}(t) = (x^{(1)}, x^{(2)}, \dots, x^{(N)}) \in \mathbb{R}^{nN}_+ .

Mass closure applies globally, but each compartment may temporarily accumulate or deplete materials via inter‑compartment flows. Let the stoichiometric composition matrix A \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times n} be the same for all compartments. The global mass conservation is

\sum_{\ell=1}^{N} A x^{(\ell)}(t) = M_{\text{total}} \in \mathbb{R}^m_+ \quad \forall t \ge 0 .

The dynamics within a compartment \ell are governed by

\dot{x}^{(\ell)} = f_\ell(x^{(\ell)}) + G_\ell(x^{(\ell)}) u^{(\ell)} + \sum_{j \neq \ell} \left[ \Phi_{\ell j}(x^{(j)}, x^{(\ell)}) - \Phi_{j\ell}(x^{(\ell)}, x^{(j)}) \right],

where \Phi_{\ell j} is a transport operator representing advection, diffusion, or active pumping of materials from compartment j to \ell. These operators must respect element‑wise conservation, i.e. A \Phi_{\ell j} = - A \Phi_{j\ell}. The full system can be viewed as a networked dynamical system on a graph, with local control inputs u^{(\ell)} \in U_\ell(x^{(\ell)}) and possibly inter‑compartment valve controls.

The ecological state space is thus the product of non‑negative orthants intersected with the global mass‑conservation hyperplane:

\mathcal{M}_{\text{net}} = \left\{ \mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{nN}_+ \;\middle|\; \sum_{\ell=1}^N A x^{(\ell)} = M_{\text{total}} \right\},

a compact convex polytope of dimension nN - m (assuming full‑rank constraints).

3.2 Viability Domain and Controlled Dynamics

Habitability requires that in every compartment \ell a set of local constraints be satisfied:

g_k^{(\ell)}(x^{(\ell)}) \ge 0, \quad k = 1,\dots,p_\ell .

The global viability domain is the product set

K = \prod_{\ell=1}^N K_\ell, \quad K_\ell = \{ x^{(\ell)} \in \mathbb{R}^n_+ \mid g_k^{(\ell)}(x^{(\ell)}) \ge 0,\; \forall k \}.

We define a global nonsmooth barrier function

h(\mathbf{x}) = \min_{\ell, k} g_k^{(\ell)}(x^{(\ell)}),

so that K = \{ \mathbf{x} \in \mathcal{M}_{\text{net}} \mid h(\mathbf{x}) \ge 0 \}. The dynamics of the entire network take the compact form

\dot{\mathbf{x}} = \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}) + \mathbf{G}(\mathbf{x}) \mathbf{u}, \quad \mathbf{u} \in \mathbf{U}(\mathbf{x}) = \prod_{\ell} U_\ell(x^{(\ell)}),

where \mathbf{f} aggregates the local autonomous dynamics and transport terms, and \mathbf{G} distributes the compartmental controls.

3.3 Nonsmooth Viability and Control Barrier Functions

The viability kernel \operatorname{Viab}_{\mathcal{F}}(K) is the set of initial states from which there exists a measurable control policy keeping the global trajectory in K for all future time. At a boundary point where two or more constraints are active (a common occurrence in networked, multi‑compartment systems), the barrier function h is nondifferentiable. We therefore employ the nonsmooth control barrier function framework (Glotfelter, Cortés, & Egerstedt, 2017), where the Dini directional derivative

h'(\mathbf{x}; \mathbf{v}) = \min_{(\ell,k) \in I(\mathbf{x})} \langle \nabla g_k^{(\ell)}(x^{(\ell)}), \mathbf{v} \rangle,

with I(\mathbf{x}) = \{ (\ell,k) \mid g_k^{(\ell)}(x^{(\ell)}) = h(\mathbf{x}) \}, replaces the gradient.

The central safety condition for forward invariance of K is therefore: there exists a control \mathbf{u} \in \mathbf{U}(\mathbf{x}) such that

\min_{(\ell,k) \in I(\mathbf{x})} \langle \nabla g_k^{(\ell)}, \dot{x}^{(\ell)} \rangle \ge - \alpha(h(\mathbf{x})),

for some extended class‑\mathcal{K} function \alpha. In terms of the dynamics, this is the sup‑min condition

\sup_{\mathbf{u} \in \mathbf{U}(\mathbf{x})} \min_{(\ell,k) \in I(\mathbf{x})} \left\langle \nabla g_k^{(\ell)}, f_\ell + G_\ell u^{(\ell)} + \sum_{j \neq \ell} (\Phi_{\ell j} - \Phi_{j\ell}) \right\rangle \ge -\alpha(h(\mathbf{x})).
\tag{2}

3.4 The Minimum Viable Biosphere Theorem (Corrected, Networked Version)

Minimum Viable Biosphere Theorem (sufficiency, networked).
Given a networked compartmental biosphere with global viability domain K = \{ \mathbf{x} \mid h(\mathbf{x}) \ge 0 \} and dynamics as above. If there exists an extended class‑\mathcal{K} function \alpha such that (2) holds for all \mathbf{x} \in K, then K is forward invariant and the viability kernel contains \operatorname{int}(K). Consequently, any initial state \mathbf{x}_{\text{init}} \in \operatorname{int}(K) admits a safe control policy.

Proof. The proof mirrors the corrected single‑compartment case. The inequality (2) ensures the set of safe controls is non‑empty at every state. A Filippov‑type selection yields a measurable feedback. Along the resulting trajectory, the absolute continuity of h(\mathbf{x}(t)) and the Dini derivative bound \dot{h} \ge -\alpha(h) guarantee, via the comparison lemma, that h never becomes negative. ∎

This theorem extends the certification concept to distributed habitats. However, the condition (2) is infinite‑dimensional and non‑convex in general, requiring the computational strategies that follow.

