5. Tractable Functional Redundancy Calculus

The Functional Redundancy Index measures the minimum number of species whose simultaneous extinction would cause the vector of critical biogeochemical outputs V = F s to leave a safe set \mathcal{V}. Computing this exactly requires examining all subsets, which is NP‑hard. We replace it with a convex relaxation that yields a polynomial‑time lower bound — a safe underestimation that is still useful for certification.

Formally, let \mathcal{S} be a set of species with current population vector s. The effect of removing a subset of species corresponds to zeroing some components. We can model this via a binary mask z \in \{0,1\}^k where z_i = 0 means species i is lost. The output after loss is V(z) = F (s \odot z). The set \mathcal{V} is defined by linear inequalities C V \ge d (e.g., minimum oxygen production). The minimum extinction cardinality is the solution to

\min_{z \in \{0,1\}^k} \sum_i (1 - z_i) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad C F (s \odot z) \notin \mathcal{V}, \; z_i \in \{0,1\}.

Relaxing z_i \in [0,1] and noting that the constraint violation condition can be approximated by minimising the slack of the inequalities, we can formulate the redundancy index as the optimum of a convex \ell_1‑penalised problem akin to compressed sensing:

R_F^{\text{approx}}(s) = \min_{\substack{\zeta \in [0,1]^k \\ C F (s \odot (1-\zeta)) \ge d}} \|\zeta\|_1,

where \zeta_i = 1 - z_i indicates the fraction of species i lost (relaxed). The \ell_1 norm promotes sparse solutions, and the optimal value provides a computable lower bound on the true integer‑valued redundancy. Additionally, submodular optimisation techniques can be used to greedily identify the most vulnerable species combination with approximation guarantees, enabling real‑time monitoring of extinction risk.

The Functional Biodiversity Entropy is then

B(\mathbf{x}) = \left(-\sum_j p_j \ln p_j\right) \cdot \left(1 + \gamma\, R_F^{\text{approx}}(s)\right),

which now has a computable second factor even for large species pools.

