6. Digital Biosphere Twins with Mass‑Constrained Estimation

The digital twin must fuse noisy sensor data while remaining on the mass‑conservation manifold. As the manifold \mathcal{M}_{\text{net}} is an affine polytope (flat), we employ the linearly constrained Kalman filter (Simon & Chia, 2002). At each time step, the unconstrained estimate \hat{\mathbf{x}}^- is projected onto the constraint surface:

\hat{\mathbf{x}}^+ = \arg\min_{\mathbf{x} \in \mathcal{M}_{\text{net}}} (\mathbf{x} - \hat{\mathbf{x}}^-)^\top P^{-1} (\mathbf{x} - \hat{\mathbf{x}}^-),

which is a quadratic program with linear equality constraints A_{\text{global}} \mathbf{x} = M_{\text{total}} and non‑negativity. This guarantees the twin never violates atomic conservation, even during filter updates.

The estimated state is then fed into the viability condition (3) in a model‑predictive control fashion: at each time step, we verify that a safe control exists and, if not, alert the crew or trigger reconfiguration. The framework thus enables a real‑time safety oracle.

