7. Computational Implementation Strategy: Step‑by‑Step Plan

To transform the theoretical certification condition into a functional engineering tool, the following practical steps will be undertaken during the PhD.

Step 1: Low‑Dimensional Prototype (MELiSSA analogue)

· Build a reduced‑order model of a closed life‑support loop with 4–5 functional compartments (e.g., crew, higher plants, nitrifying bioreactor, waste digester).
· Define explicit stoichiometric matrices and physiological constraints (O₂, CO₂, nitrogen species).
· Compute the viability kernel exactly using a dynamic‑programming grid method in 3–4 dimensions to serve as ground truth for later approximations.

Step 2: Neural Barrier Function Approximation

· Train a physics‑informed neural network (PINN) to represent a control barrier function h_\theta(\mathbf{x}) that satisfies the sup‑min inequality (2) over a sampled domain.
· Use the exact kernel from Step 1 to validate the neural approximation accuracy. Benchmark against grid methods.

Step 3: Scale to Networked Compartmental Model

· Expand the model to a 3‑compartment network (e.g., crew, greenhouse, air handler) with inter‑compartment diffusion and active ventilation.
· Derive a global safety condition by composing local NCBFs using Boolean AND composition (min), leveraging the nonsmooth CBF theory.
· Verify through simulation that safety is maintained even when local gradients would cause failure in a lumped model.

Step 4: Introduce Epistemic Uncertainty and Robustness

· Add a bounded model mismatch term \Delta to key ecological parameters (e.g., respiration rate, photosynthetic efficiency).
· Train a robust NCBF by incorporating the worst‑case term \delta\|\nabla g\| into the loss function.
· Demonstrate that the robust controller keeps the system safe while a non‑robust one fails under parameter drift.

Step 5: Implement Tractable Redundancy Monitoring

· For a synthetic microbial community (e.g., nitrogen‑cycling guilds), implement the \ell_1 convex relaxation of the redundancy index.
· Compute R_F^{\text{approx}} in real time as species populations vary; validate against exhaustive search for small communities.
· Integrate the index into the digital twin dashboard as a “functional resilience meter.”

Step 6: Historical Validation on Biosphere 2 Data

· Reconstruct a compartmental model of Biosphere 2’s terrestrial biome and technical basement, parameterised from literature data.
· Identify the oxygen decline episode as a violation of the stoichiometric manifold due to concrete carbonation (unmodeled CO₂ sink).
· Demonstrate that a BAM‑style viability kernel, if computed a priori, would have predicted the unsafe trajectory and triggered an early warning.

Step 7: End‑to‑End Digital Twin Demo

· Integrate the constrained Kalman filter, robust NCBF safety monitor, and redundancy index into a single software framework.
· Run closed‑loop simulations with random disturbances and sensor noise; show that the digital twin correctly identifies safe vs. unsafe states and recommends control actions that preserve viability.

This step‑by‑step plan ensures that the mathematical theory is incrementally grounded in computational feasibility and empirical relevance.

