8. Validation Case Studies

Case Study 1: Biosphere 2 Oxygen Decline. Biosphere 2 (1991–1993) experienced an unexpected drop in atmospheric O₂ from 21% to 14% and a concurrent rise in CO₂. Post‑hoc analysis attributed much of the O₂ loss to chemical binding with curing concrete in the structure, an unanticipated sink. In our validation, we will model the system as a two‑compartment network (biome + concrete structure), explicitly tracking carbon and oxygen atoms via the stoichiometric manifold. We will show that the pre‑mission viability kernel, constructed from expected biological rates, would have been rendered empty by the concrete sink — a clear certification failure. We will then demonstrate that a robust viability kernel, with an expanded set of admissible unknown sinks, would have encompassed the initial state, allowing safe operation if the control policy (e.g., ventilation, O₂ injection) had been designed accordingly.

Case Study 2: ESA MELiSSA Loop. MELiSSA is a concept for a closed regenerative life‑support system with five compartments (crew, higher plants, photobioreactor, nitrifying reactor, waste digester). Using published simulation models, we will apply BAM to certify the loop’s viability under nominal and degraded conditions, identifying critical bottlenecks (e.g., nitrifier washout) as violations of the sup‑min condition. We will demonstrate that the NCBF‑based digital twin can predict and prevent failures by pre‑emptively adjusting recycle ratios or nutrient dosing.

