Appendix A. Mathematical Preliminaries

A.1 Viability Theory and Contingent Cones

Let K \subset \mathbb{R}^n be a non‑empty closed set. The Bouligand contingent cone to K at a point x \in K is the set

T_K(x) = \left\{ v \in \mathbb{R}^n \;\middle|\; \liminf_{h \to 0^+} \frac{d_K(x+hv)}{h} = 0 \right\},

where d_K(y) = \inf_{z \in K} \|y-z\|. Equivalently, there exist sequences h_m \to 0^+ and v_m \to v such that x + h_m v_m \in K for all m.

Consider a differential inclusion \dot{x} \in \mathcal{F}(x) with \mathcal{F} upper semicontinuous with non‑empty, compact, convex values. The classical Nagumo Theorem (Nagumo, 1942) states that K is forward invariant under the inclusion if and only if

\mathcal{F}(x) \cap T_K(x) \neq \emptyset \quad \text{for all } x \in \partial K.

For the particular case K = \{x \mid g_k(x) \ge 0,\; k=1,\dots,p\} with smooth g_k, the tangent cone at a boundary point where the active constraints are I(x) = \{k \mid g_k(x)=0\} satisfies

T_K(x) \supset \{v \mid \langle \nabla g_k(x), v \rangle \ge 0 \;\forall k \in I(x) \},

and equality holds under a constraint qualification (e.g., linear independence of active gradients). In our setting, viability requires the controlled velocity \dot{x} = f(x) + G(x)u to belong to this cone, i.e. \langle \nabla g_k(x), f(x)+G(x)u \rangle \ge 0 for all k \in I(x).

A.2 Nonsmooth Analysis

A function h : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R} that is locally Lipschitz has a Clarke generalized gradient

\partial h(x) = \operatorname{conv}\left\{ \lim_{i\to\infty} \nabla h(x_i) \mid x_i \to x,\; x_i \notin \Omega_h \right\},

where \Omega_h is the set of non‑differentiability. For h(x) = \min_{k} g_k(x) with smooth g_k, we have

\partial h(x) = \operatorname{conv}\{ \nabla g_k(x) \mid k \in I(x) \}.

The Dini (directional) derivative in the direction v is

h'(x;v) = \liminf_{t \to 0^+} \frac{h(x+tv) - h(x)}{t}.

For h = \min_k g_k and x such that I(x) is the active set, one obtains

h'(x;v) = \min_{k \in I(x)} \langle \nabla g_k(x), v \rangle.

This holds because for small t, only the active g_k determine the minimum, and each g_k is differentiable. The identity is proven by comparing the increment of each g_k and noting the min persists. The function h is semiconcave in practice, and the Dini derivative serves as the natural replacement for \langle \nabla h, v \rangle in invariance conditions.

A.3 Control Barrier Functions

A Control Barrier Function (CBF) for a set K = \{x \mid h(x) \ge 0\} with a locally Lipschitz h is a function such that there exists an extended class \mathcal{K} function \alpha and, for all x \in K,

\sup_{u \in U(x)} \dot{h}(x; f(x)+G(x)u) \ge -\alpha(h(x)),

where \dot{h} denotes the appropriate lower Dini derivative along the controlled vector field. When h is smooth, \dot{h} = \langle \nabla h, f+Gu \rangle. In the nonsmooth case (Glotfelter et al., 2017), the condition becomes

\sup_{u \in U(x)} \min_{v \in \partial h(x)} \langle v, f(x)+G(x)u \rangle \ge -\alpha(h(x)),

which for h = \min g_k reduces to \sup_u \min_{k \in I(x)} \langle \nabla g_k, f+Gu \rangle \ge -\alpha(h(x)). This is the sup‑min condition used in the Minimum Viable Biosphere Theorem.

If such a control exists for every x \in K, then K is forward invariant under the feedback policy selecting u that meets the inequality (see proof below).

