Appendix C. Networked Compartmental Extension

C.1 Transport‑Coupled Dynamics

Let the biosphere be partitioned into N compartments. For compartment \ell, the state x^{(\ell)} \in \mathbb{R}^n_+ evolves as

\dot{x}^{(\ell)} = f_\ell(x^{(\ell)}) + G_\ell(x^{(\ell)}) u^{(\ell)} + \sum_{j \neq \ell} \left[ \Phi_{\ell j}(x^{(j)}, x^{(\ell)}) - \Phi_{j\ell}(x^{(\ell)}, x^{(j)}) \right],

where \Phi_{\ell j} : \mathbb{R}^n_+ \times \mathbb{R}^n_+ \to \mathbb{R}^n_+ is the transport flux from compartment j to \ell. Global mass closure demands that for each element, the net exchange sums to zero, i.e., A \Phi_{\ell j} + A \Phi_{j\ell} = 0, and consequently the total mass vector M_{\text{total}} = \sum_\ell A x^{(\ell)} is conserved:

\frac{d}{dt} \sum_\ell A x^{(\ell)} = \sum_\ell A \dot{x}^{(\ell)} = \sum_\ell A \left[ f_\ell + G_\ell u^{(\ell)} \right] + \sum_\ell \sum_{j \neq \ell} A \left[ \Phi_{\ell j} - \Phi_{j\ell} \right] = 0,

because the double sum vanishes pairwise and A f_\ell = 0, A G_\ell = 0 by design. Thus \mathbf{x}(t) stays on the affine manifold \mathcal{M}_{\text{net}}.

C.2 Viability Domain and Barrier Function

Each compartment has local constraints g_k^{(\ell)}(x^{(\ell)}) \ge 0. The global viability domain is K = \prod_{\ell=1}^N K_\ell with K_\ell = \{x^{(\ell)} \mid g_k^{(\ell)}(x^{(\ell)}) \ge 0, \forall k\}. We define the global barrier function

h(\mathbf{x}) = \min_{\ell, k} g_k^{(\ell)}(x^{(\ell)}).

The active index set is I(\mathbf{x}) = \{(\ell,k) \mid g_k^{(\ell)}(x^{(\ell)}) = h(\mathbf{x})\}. The Dini derivative of h in a direction \mathbf{v} = (v^{(1)},\dots,v^{(N)}) is

h'(\mathbf{x}; \mathbf{v}) = \min_{(\ell,k) \in I(\mathbf{x})} \langle \nabla g_k^{(\ell)}(x^{(\ell)}), v^{(\ell)} \rangle.

C.3 Networked Minimum Viable Biosphere Theorem

Theorem (Networked MVB). Assume the dynamics above and the global barrier h. If there exists an extended class \mathcal{K} function \alpha such that for all \mathbf{x} \in K,

\sup_{\mathbf{u} \in \mathbf{U}(\mathbf{x})} \min_{(\ell,k) \in I(\mathbf{x})} \left\langle \nabla g_k^{(\ell)}, f_\ell + G_\ell u^{(\ell)} + \sum_{j \neq \ell} (\Phi_{\ell j} - \Phi_{j\ell}) \right\rangle \ge -\alpha(h(\mathbf{x})), \tag{C.1}

then K is forward invariant and any initial state in its interior admits a viable control policy.

Proof. The construction mirrors Appendix B. For each \mathbf{x}, define

\Psi_{\text{net}}(\mathbf{x}) = \left\{ \mathbf{u} \in \mathbf{U}(\mathbf{x}) \mid \min_{(\ell,k) \in I(\mathbf{x})} \left\langle \nabla g_k^{(\ell)}, f_\ell + G_\ell u^{(\ell)} + \text{transport} \right\rangle \ge -\alpha(h(\mathbf{x})) \right\}.

Compactness and upper semicontinuity follow from the same arguments. A measurable selection \mathbf{u} = \kappa(\mathbf{x}) \in \Psi_{\text{net}}(\mathbf{x}) exists. Along the solution, the Dini derivative of h satisfies

\dot{h}(\mathbf{x}(t)) \ge \min_{(\ell,k) \in I(\mathbf{x})} \left\langle \nabla g_k^{(\ell)}, \dot{x}^{(\ell)} \right\rangle \ge -\alpha(h(\mathbf{x}(t))),

where the last inequality uses the definition of \kappa. The comparison lemma again yields h \ge 0. ∎

Thus, the networked theorem reduces to verifying a sup‑min inequality that couples the local controls and transport terms. This condition is the basis for scalable neural approximations.

