Appendix E. Robust Control Barrier Functions

E.1 Model Uncertainty

To account for unmodeled dynamics, we write the true system as

\dot{x} = f(x) + G(x)u + \Delta(x,t),

where the perturbation \Delta(x,t) satisfies \|\Delta(x,t)\| \le \delta for all x \in K, t \ge 0, and \delta is a known constant. (For networked case, each compartment may have its own perturbation with known bound.)

E.2 Robust CBF Condition

We augment the safety condition (B.1) to require that the worst‑case perturbation cannot drive the state out of K. The Dini derivative of the barrier function becomes, under control u and any admissible \Delta,

h'(x; f+Gu+\Delta) = \min_{k \in I(x)} \langle \nabla g_k, f+Gu+\Delta \rangle.

We need the bound

\min_{k \in I(x)} \langle \nabla g_k, f+Gu+\Delta \rangle \ge -\alpha(h(x)) \quad \forall \|\Delta\| \le \delta.

Since the minimum over k is a concave function, the worst‑case over \Delta can be computed by minimizing the inner product over \Delta. The condition

\min_{k \in I(x)} \inf_{\|\Delta\| \le \delta} \langle \nabla g_k, \Delta \rangle = \min_{k \in I(x)} \bigl( \langle \nabla g_k, f+Gu \rangle - \delta \|\nabla g_k\| \bigr).

Hence a Robust Control Barrier Function (RCBF) condition is

\sup_{u \in U(x)} \min_{k \in I(x)} \bigl[ \langle \nabla g_k, f(x)+G(x)u \rangle - \delta \|\nabla g_k(x)\| \bigr] \ge -\alpha(h(x)). \tag{E.1}

If this holds, then for the selected control u,

\dot{h}(x(t)) \ge \min_{k \in I(x)} \langle \nabla g_k, \dot{x} \rangle \ge \min_{k \in I(x)} [ \langle \nabla g_k, f+Gu \rangle - \delta \|\nabla g_k\| ] \ge -\alpha(h),

and by the same comparison argument, forward invariance is preserved under all admissible uncertainties.

E.3 Adaptive Tightening

When the uncertainty bound \delta is initially conservative, online parameter estimation (e.g., using the digital twin’s constrained Kalman filter) can produce a tighter bound \hat{\delta}(t) over time. The RCBF condition can then be re‑evaluated with the reduced \hat{\delta}, decreasing the required control effort. The adaptive scheme uses a time‑varying barrier condition and retains safety as long as the bound is valid.

