Appendix F. Convex Relaxation of Functional Redundancy Index

F.1 Exact Problem

Let s \in \mathbb{R}^k_+ be the species population vector, F \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times k} the trait matrix, and \mathcal{V} = \{ V \in \mathbb{R}^m \mid C V \ge d \} the safe functional output set (polyhedral). The exact minimum number of extinctions that breach safety is:

R_F(s) = \min_{z \in \{0,1\}^k} \sum_{i=1}^k (1 - z_i) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad C F (s \odot z) \not\ge d,

or equivalently, \exists j such that (C F (s \odot z))_j < d_j. This is a combinatorial minimum‑cardinality disruption problem and is NP‑hard in general.

F.2 ℓ₁ Convex Relaxation

Introduce the continuous variable \zeta_i = 1 - z_i \in [0,1], representing the fraction of species i removed. The safe condition after fractional removal is C F (s \odot (1-\zeta)) \ge d. To find the minimum number of whole‑species extinctions needed to violate safety, we can relax to a sparsity‑inducing convex program:

R_F^{\text{relax}}(s) = \min_{\zeta \in [0,1]^k} \|\zeta\|_1 \quad \text{s.t.} \quad C F (s \odot (1-\zeta)) \ge d. \tag{F.1}

This is a linear program (since \|\zeta\|_1 = \sum \zeta_i for \zeta \ge 0) with linear constraints. Any feasible integer solution \zeta \in \{0,1\}^k corresponds to a feasible extinction set; hence the minimum of the LP provides a lower bound on the true redundancy:

R_F^{\text{relax}}(s) \le R_F(s).

Thus, a large value of the relaxation indicates high redundancy; a small value warns of vulnerability. Moreover, a rounding heuristic can extract a feasible integer solution, and the integrality gap is typically small for such problems when the constraint matrix is well‑conditioned. The LP can be solved in polynomial time, enabling real‑time monitoring.

F.3 Submodular Formulation

Alternatively, define a set function f(S) as the minimum slack of the constraints after removing species subset S, or as the indicator of safety violation. While not fully submodular in all cases, under certain monotonicity assumptions (species contributions are non‑negative and independent), the problem exhibits submodularity. A greedy algorithm then provides a (1-1/e)‑approximate guarantee to the maximum safe set size. We leave detailed investigation to future work; the LP relaxation is our primary tractable tool.

